\description{
\code{emoji_coverage()} reports, for each lexicon, how many of the emoji in a
text column it can score: as a share of the emoji \emph{occurrences}, as a share
of the distinct emoji \emph{types}, and as the number of emoji-bearing rows it
leaves without any score at all. It is the figure to report beside any
lexicon-based emoji result, because a lexicon that scores the common emoji
and none of the rare ones can look complete on one of those measures and
patchy on the other.
}
\details{
\strong{Report two numbers, never one.} Emoji use is heavily skewed: a few
glyphs make up most occurrences, and a long tail of glyphs turns up once or
twice each. So a lexicon built from the common glyphs covers most
\emph{occurrences} and few \emph{types}, and the two figures answer different
questions:
\itemize{
\item \code{occurrence_coverage}: what share of the emoji a reader of this corpus
saw can be scored. This is the flattering one.
\item \code{type_coverage}: what share of the emoji vocabulary of this corpus can
be scored. This is the one that says whether the rare, domain-specific
glyphs are measured at all.
}

On the example corpus, \link{oasst_messages}, the two differ by fourteen points
for the bundled sentiment lexicon, 75.0\% of occurrences against 61.3\% of
types, and the third number is the one with consequences: 135 of the 750
rows that hold an emoji have none the lexicon can score, so their
\code{.emoji_sentiment} is \code{NA}. Dropping those rows before a comparison drops
a sixth of the data, and not at random.

\code{lexicon_size} and \code{catalogue_share} describe the lexicon rather than the
corpus: how many distinct emoji it scores, and what share of the emoji in
the catalogue those are. Skin-tone and hair components are not emoji
occurrences (see the \emph{Detection} section of \link{tidyEmoji}) and are left out
of the catalogue's count.

A registered lexicon can score an emoji when it holds a value for it in a
column the scoring verbs read by default: the score \code{\link[=emoji_score]{emoji_score()}} and
\code{\link[=emoji_sentiment]{emoji_sentiment()}} take (\code{sentiment_score}, else \code{score}), or an emotion
column \code{\link[=emoji_emotion]{emoji_emotion()}} takes. Its other numeric columns, such as a count
of raters, do not count. A score chosen with \verb{score =} can cover a
different set.
